      "The Maillard reaction is named after French chemist Louis-Camille Maillard, who first described it in 1912. It occurs when amino acids and reducing sugars are exposed to heat — typically above 140°C (285°F) — and react to produce hundreds of different flavour and aroma compounds simultaneously. It is not a single reaction but a cascade of hundreds of interconnected ones, which is why browned food is so much more complex and interesting than unbrowned food.",
      "The key insight for cooking is this: the Maillard reaction requires both heat and a relatively dry surface. Water boils at 100°C, which is below the temperature needed for browning. This means a wet or damp surface will spend all its energy evaporating moisture before it can brown. This is why patting meat dry before searing is so important — any moisture on the surface must first evaporate before the Maillard reaction can begin. A wet steak steams rather than sears.",
