      "It is difficult to overstate how valuable spices were in medieval Europe. Pepper, cloves, nutmeg, cinnamon and cardamom were worth their weight in silver and sometimes gold. Pepper was used as currency and to pay ransoms. When the Visigoths sacked Rome in 410 CE, their ransom demand included 3,000 pounds of pepper. The entire infrastructure of medieval European commerce — the merchant republics of Venice and Genoa, the rise of banking, the first joint-stock companies — was built substantially on the spice trade.",
      "The reason European spices were so expensive was geography. They grew in the islands of Southeast Asia, in India and in Sri Lanka. To reach Europe, they travelled overland through Central Asia and the Middle East or by sea through the Indian Ocean and the Persian Gulf, passing through dozens of middlemen — Arab, Persian, Indian and Ottoman merchants — each taking a margin. By the time pepper reached Lisbon or Venice, its price had increased a hundredfold from its origin.",
