      "The Columbian Exchange — the transfer of plants, animals and diseases between the Americas and the rest of the world after 1492 — is the single most transformative event in culinary history. From the Americas came tomatoes, potatoes, maize, chillies, chocolate, vanilla, peanuts, squash, beans, and turkey. Within 200 years of Columbus, these ingredients had been adopted and adapted into cuisines across Europe, Africa and Asia. Today it is impossible to imagine Italian cooking without tomatoes, Indian cooking without chillies, Irish cooking without potatoes, or Thai cooking without chillies and peanuts — yet all of these ingredients arrived in those cuisines only after 1500.",
      "The spice trade also drove the development of the world's most complex spice-blending traditions. Indian masalas, Moroccan ras el hanout, Ethiopian berbere, the baharat of the Arab world — these sophisticated spice blends developed in cultures that sat along the trading routes and had access to the widest variety of spices. The more spices available, the more complex the blending tradition that developed around them.",
      "The legacy of the spice trade is written into every cuisine on earth. The chilli in your Korean kimchi, the vanilla in your French crème brûlée, the black pepper in your Italian pasta — all are products of trade routes that began over 2,000 years ago. Modern globalisation of food is not a new phenomenon. It is the continuation of a process that has been reshaping what humanity eats since the first merchant loaded cinnamon onto a ship.",
