      "The practical cooking implications are significant. Umami compounds are particularly concentrated in aged, fermented, dried and slow-cooked foods. Parmesan aged for 24 months has far more glutamate than young cheese. Anchovies that have been salt-cured for a year are umami bombs. A tomato paste cooked down until it darkens has more umami than fresh tomatoes. Fish sauce, made from fermented anchovies, is perhaps the most concentrated source of umami in any cuisine.",
      "Synergy is the most powerful umami concept for cooks: certain combinations of umami compounds produce dramatically more savouriness together than the sum of their parts. Glutamate (in tomatoes, parmesan, mushrooms) combined with inosinate (in meat and fish) produces a synergistic effect that multiplies the perceived umami five to eight times. This is why bolognese tastes richer than minced beef alone, why parmesan on pasta is transformative, and why the Japanese combination of kombu (glutamate) and bonito (inosinate) in dashi produces such an extraordinarily savoury stock from just two ingredients.",
